Add({
	id = "elwynn-forest",
	title = "Elwynn Forest",
	aliases = { "Elwynn" },
	faction = "Alliance",
	continent = "Eastern Kingdoms",
	maps = { 1429, 37 },
	zone = "Elwynn Forest",
	unlock = {
		{ type = "zone" },
		{ type = "map" },
	},
	regionKind = "zone",
	kind = "region",
	sections = {
		{
			title = "Summary",
			text = [=[Elwynn Forest is the green heart of the {link:kingdom-of-stormwind}Kingdom of Stormwind{/link}. Its oaks crowd close about the white walls of the capital, and between them lie quiet lakes, golden fields and old mines that have fed the kingdom for generations. To the folk of {link:stormwind-city}Stormwind{/link}, Elwynn has always been the safest place in the world. That belief is now wearing thin. Wolves prowl nearer the farms each season, strange things stir in the mines, and the roads that once knew only merchants and pilgrims now know thieves.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Origins",
			text = [=[No chronicle records the year when the first humans settled Elwynn. What is known is that the forest was claimed many centuries before the {link:dark-portal}Dark Portal{/link} opened. When the ancient empire of {link:arathor}Arathor{/link} ended its long unity, bold families of the {link:arathi-highlands}Arathi{/link} bloodline travelled south in search of new lands. Among the rich woods and rivers they found there, they raised a kingdom they named {link:azeroth}Azeroth{/link}, and at its heart a city called {link:stormwind-city}Stormwind{/link}. Elwynn became the realm's granary and its garden. North of the city, the clerics of {link:northshire-abbey}Northshire Abbey{/link} tended the faith of the {link:holy-light}Holy Light{/link}, and their abbey became one of its oldest homes in the south.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "The Present Day",
			text = [=[Elwynn has regrown from the ashes of war, but it is poorly guarded. King {link:varian-wrynn}Varian Wrynn{/link} has vanished, and none can say where he has gone. In his absence, Highlord {link:bolvar-fordragon}Bolvar Fordragon{/link} rules as regent, and the strength of the realm is spread thin across a dozen troubled borders. {link:kobolds}Kobolds{/link} have seized the {link:fargodeep-mine}Fargodeep{/link} and {link:jasperlode-mine}Jasperlode{/link} mines. {link:riverpaw-gnolls}Riverpaw gnolls{/link} raid across the southern border. Bandits of the {link:defias-brotherhood}Defias Brotherhood{/link} prey upon the farmsteads near {link:westfall}Westfall{/link}. The few marshals of {link:goldshire}Goldshire{/link} can no longer hold the forest alone, and they welcome any sword willing to serve.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Inhabitants",
			text = [=[• {b}The people of {link:stormwind-city}Stormwind{/link}.{/b} Farmers, the loggers of {link:eastvale-logging-camp}Eastvale{/link} and the miners of the hills remain because the land is good, and because they have nowhere else to go.
• {b}The clergy and recruits of {link:northshire-abbey}Northshire{/link}.{/b} At the abbey, priests and paladins train the young men and women who will defend the kingdom.
• {b}{link:kobolds}Kobolds{/link}.{/b} They burrow in the mines. They fear the dark above all things and guard their candles with savage devotion.
• {b}The {link:riverpaw-gnolls}Riverpaw gnolls{/link}.{/b} They come down from the southern hills and from {link:redridge-mountains}Redridge{/link}, hungry for food and plunder.
• {b}The {link:defias-brotherhood}Defias Brotherhood{/link}.{/b} Their ranks began with men who once rebuilt Stormwind, and who now take by the knife what they believe the crown still owes them.
• {b}Murlocs.{/b} The {link:murlocs}murloc{/link} tribes haunt the shores of {link:crystal-lake}Crystal Lake{/link} and {link:stone-cairn-lake}Stone Cairn Lake{/link}, as they have for as long as anyone can remember.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "History",
			text = [=[• {b}The Long Peace.{/b} For generations Elwynn knew little but harvest and festival. Its villages grew rich on the trade that flowed through {link:stormwind-city}Stormwind{/link}.
• {b}The First War.{/b} Then the orcish {link:horde}Horde{/link} came through the {link:dark-portal}Dark Portal{/link}, and the lands of {link:azeroth}Azeroth{/link} were the first to burn. In the {link:first-war}First War{/link}, the Horde put Elwynn's farms and forests to the torch, and even the sacred halls of {link:northshire-abbey}Northshire Abbey{/link} did not escape the flames. At last Stormwind itself was sacked, and King {link:llane-wrynn}Llane Wrynn{/link} was slain within his own keep. Sir {link:anduin-lothar}Anduin Lothar{/link} led the survivors north across the sea to {link:lordaeron}Lordaeron{/link}, and with them went the king's young son, {link:varian-wrynn}Varian{/link}.
• {b}The Return.{/b} After the {link:second-war}Second War{/link}, the exiles came home. They replanted the forest and rebuilt the villages. The {link:stonemasons-guild}Stonemasons' Guild{/link}, led by {link:edwin-vancleef}Edwin VanCleef{/link}, raised Stormwind anew from its rubble. When the {link:house-of-nobles}House of Nobles{/link} refused to pay the masons for their labor, the masons rioted in the streets. Queen {link:tiffin-wrynn}Tiffin Wrynn{/link} was among those who died in the chaos. The masons were driven from the city, and from their bitterness the {link:defias-brotherhood}Defias Brotherhood{/link} was born.
• {b}The Present Day.{/b} Now the king is gone, the armies are far away, and Elwynn waits on the courage of a handful of marshals and volunteers.]=],
		},
		{
			title = "Lore of the Forest",
			text = [=[• Among the kobolds of Elwynn, no crime is greater than stealing a candle. Every miner who has worked below knows the cry that follows such a theft.
• The Maclure and Stonefield families have feuded across the same fence for longer than either can remember. Yet the whispers of {link:goldshire}Goldshire{/link} say that a Maclure daughter and a Stonefield son meet in secret when the fields are dark.
• The {link:tower-of-azora}Tower of Azora{/link} rises in the east of the forest. It is the home of a mage whose studies the common folk of Elwynn neither understand nor care to ask about.
• The farmers of the south speak of {link:hogger}Hogger{/link} in low voices. He is a {link:gnolls}gnoll{/link} so large and cunning that the bounty on his head has gone unclaimed through many seasons.]=],
		},
	},
})

